  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "$\\frac{1}{10}\\times \\frac{6}{7}=$",
  "worked_solution": "We need to solve the following multiplication problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{10}\\times \\frac{6}{7}\n$$\n\nNotice that the first fraction's denominator ($10$) and the second fraction's numerator ($6$) have a common \nfactor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we can simplify our problem by first swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{6}{10}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the second fraction to its lowest terms by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{6÷2}{10÷2} & =\\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{1\\times 3}{7\\times 5}=\\frac{3}{35}\n$$",
